  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two transport mechanisms around active particles or reactions in fluid. On the left, “entrainment” is illustrated for a swimmer moving horizontally. A blue curve marks how tracer displacement depends on distance from the swimmer’s path, with a critical distance \\(d_c\\) indicated. Red example trajectories show that tracers farther away follow loop-like paths and end up with only a small backward shift, whereas tracers starting closer than \\(d_c\\) are swept along and experience a large, forward displacement that grows strongly near the path. Overall, the plot emphasizes a sharp transition from weak far-field motion to strong near-field entrainment.\n\nOn the right, “convection” is shown schematically in two fluid layers. Circular arrows represent recirculating flow cells driven by a localized reaction. When the reaction occurs at the bottom, the circulation remains concentrated near the lower region. When the reaction occurs at the interface, reactants also enter from above, indicated by downward dashed arrows labeled “reactant influx,” and the convective mixing appears to distribute particles more broadly through the volume. Taken together, the figure highlights how both swimmer-induced flow and reaction location control transport and mixing in fluids.",
